Interesting. The slot test file references a TODO: "Unskip when the underlying issue is resolved - Reverted in https://github.com/radix-ui/primitives/pull/3554". There's a test for `given an Input`, which expects that the function ref is called only once even when typing 'foo'. This is the classic bug: when you type in an input that uses Slot (asChild), the Slot regenerates the result of `composeRefs(...)` on every render, producing a new function identity. In React 19, when a ref callback's identity changes, React calls the old ref with null and the new ref with the node... wait, no. In React 19, ref callbacks are only called with null/again when identity changes AND the effect is... no, actually.

Hold on — the actual known issue is: in Slot, on every render `composeRefs(forwardedRef, slottableElementRef)` is called inline (not memoized). Since `composeRefs` creates a new function on each render, React sees a "changed" ref and calls cleanup (old ref with null) and the new ref with the node again. This shouldn't by itself cause a loop, but... in React 19, when a ref callback returns a cleanup function, React treats that as an effect (React 19 uses an effect for ref callbacks?). Hmm.

