Here's what I've figured out now:

```html
<span data-radix-focus-guard ...></span>
<div>                              ← render container
  <div data-tick="0">              ← my harness div
    <button data-aria-hidden="true" aria-hidden="true">tick</button>   ← my button, aria-hidden!
    <div data-radix-popper-content-wrapper ...>
      <div role="menu" ...>       ← menu content (inside container?!)
```

Two realizations:

1. **`MenuContent` isn't portaled here** — it's rendered inline (the popper content wrapper is inside the harness div). (Maybe in this fork the default container for Portal is the parent element, or MenuContent renders inline by default... whatever, it doesn't matter.)

2. **My tick button has become `aria-hidden="true"`!** The menu is **modal** (in this fork the default for `modal` in MenuContent seems to be true), and the modal logic sets `aria-hidden` on everything *outside* the modal content (Radix Modal behavior: `handleAriaHidden` — hides siblings of the modal layer). So `getByRole('button', { name: 'tick' })` fails by default... no wait, testing-library's `getByRole` should find `hidden: false` — aria-hidden elements are filtered out of the a11y tree. So that's why it's not found!

**Fix for the probe**: instead of clicking the button, I should re-render the harness. The harness holds state internally, and `rerender()` from render doesn't change the internal state... but I can restructure: hold `tick` in an external mutable that's read during render, and call `rerender()` — React will re-render Harness (with new props / or forceUpdate), and the state read inside... the simplest is:

```tsx
let force = 0;
const Harness = () => { force; /* read */ return (...uses force as data-tick...); };
const view = render(<Harness />);
force += 1;
view.rerender(<Harness />);
```
Reading the external `force` during render and re-rendering with the same element type will re-render Harness (React re-renders the components in the tree on rerender — since Harness is a function component, rerender causes it to be called again). The data-tick attribute changes (force read during render) — this triggers the re-render chain: Harness → Menu (root) re-renders (new element) → context value new → MenuSubTrigger re-renders → churn in pre-fix.

Wait — does `rerender(<Harness/>)` re-render Harness? Yes: the new element is reconciled, the component re-renders (props are the same, but the parent (root) re-renders → child re-renders unless memoized). ✓

And the a11y-hidden problem is avoided too: we never query the tick button; we query the sub trigger (a menuitem — inside the modal content, visible) ✓ (the mount assertion already passes, since subTrigger was found).

